{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"defiable-distortive-pinniped","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n`scripts/gen_env_registry.py` is now fail-CLOSED for `os.environ` accesses (bug\n`blocky-matricidal-squid`, Gerrit 1979): an attribute it cannot classify aborts the\ngenerator naming the site. The OTHER half of the same blind spot is untouched and still\nfails OPEN.\n\n`KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` maps a project env-read helper name to the argument position holding\nthe env-var name. A helper ABSENT from that table is not an error -- it is invisible. The\nscan walks past every call to it, the drift gate stays green, and the variables it reads\nare simply missing from `docs/env-vars.md`.\n\nThis is not speculative. It has already cost documentation once: bug\n`b00f-c62e-d3b9-4e63` (scared-restored-swallow) lost FOUR undocumented vars to a missing\n`_llm_float` row, and the fix added the row while explicitly recording that the CLASS\nstayed open. The comment sits in the file today:\n\n    Omitting this one cost four undocumented vars (bug b00f): a helper absent from this\n    table is not an error, it is INVISIBLE -- the scan walks past every call and the drift\n    gate stays green, so a clean `--check` proves agreement with the generator, never\n    completeness.\n\nSo the generator now has two different failure postures for the same underlying hazard:\nan unknown `os.environ` accessor stops the build, while an unknown helper is silent.\n\n## Why it is harder than the os.environ half\n`os.environ` is a single named object, so \"every attribute access on it\" is a closed set an\nAST pass can enumerate and classify. A helper is an arbitrary local function; there is no\nsyntactic marker that says \"this one reads the environment\". Any fix has to invent that\nsignal. Sketches, to be decided on evidence rather than picked from this list:\n- an explicit decorator or naming convention on env-read helpers that the scan can key on\n- flagging any function whose body contains a recognised env read but whose name is not in\n  `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` -- a one-level dataflow check, catching the \"wrote a new shim and\n  forgot the row\" case that has actually happened\n- accepting fail-open here and instead asserting COVERAGE some other way\n\nNote the second option would have caught b00f exactly.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A new env-read helper added to `src/rebar` WITHOUT a `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` row produces\n      a loud failure, proven by a test that adds such a helper and asserts the failure --\n      and that FAILS against today's generator.\n- [ ] The `_llm_float`/b00f scenario is replayed as a regression test: removing a row for a\n      helper that IS used must not silently shrink the registry.\n- [ ] No false positives on the current tree: `make lint` and the full suite stay green,\n      and `docs/env-vars.md` does not change (162 keys).\n- [ ] If fail-closed is judged infeasible for helpers, record WHY on this ticket and name\n      the compensating signal instead -- silence is not an acceptable close.\n\n## Provenance\nRecorded 2026-08-20 while fixing `blocky-matricidal-squid`. Deliberately NOT folded into\nthat change: widening the os.environ recogniser is a closed, enumerable problem, and this\none needs a design decision. Filed rather than left implied-fixed by the sibling's\nfail-closed language.","id":"ff2e-339c-237e-4519","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"gen_env_registry still fails OPEN on helpers missing from KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787295226262699001,"uuid":"953b5ee1-d9a2-47f5-a8f1-304b9a5b20dd"}